   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to providing that a community college may only charge the county of a non-resident student for the local sponsor's costs for not more than two academic years while such student is in attendance",
    "basePrintNo": "S4741",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4741-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §6305, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4741\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the education law, in relation to providing that a community\ncollege may only charge the county of a non-resident student for the\nlocal sponsor's costs for not more than two academic years while such\nstudent is in attendance\n\nPURPOSE:\nTo prohibit community colleges from charging the county of residence\nof a non-resident student for local sponsor costs for more than two\nacademic years.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nSection one of the bill amends subdivision 2 of section 6305 of the\neducation law to limit the ability of community colleges to charge\nthe county of residence of a non-resident student for local sponsor\ncosts to no more than two academic years. Section two of the bill\nprovides the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nState law requires counties to reimburse each other for the tuition\ndifferential between in-county and out-of-county tuition for students\nwho attend a community college outside of their home county. This\nallows New York State residents to take advantage of resident\ntuitions at all of the State's community colleges, regardless of\nwhere in the State they reside.\n\nFor the purposes of this law, Fashion Institute of Technology (FIT) is\nconsidered as if it is a community college. It is, however, a\nfour-year school which also offers a Masters program.\nWhat's more, the unique program opportunities offered at FIT take it\nout of the realm of traditional community college missions and into\nthat of providing specific career paths and education to its\nstudents. The specific inclusion of a specialized, 4-year school in a\nprogram that was specifically designed as a two-year community\ncollege tuition program is a state mandate which places a\nconsiderable financial burden on a student's home county.\n\nIn Suffolk County, for example, the total out-of-County tuition cost\nis approximately $12 million, with FIT representing over $6.4 million\nof that total. On a per-student basis, Suffolk County's average\nper-student out-of-county tuition cost for all schools exclusive of\nFIT is $1,710, as compared to $8,681 for FIT exclusively. In summary,\nFIT accounts for more than 50% of Suffolk's total out-of-county\ntuition cost, while only 18% of out-of-county students attend.\n\nThe inclusion of a specialized, four-year school in a program\nspecifically designed as a two-year community college tuition program\nis an aberration that unduly burdens county taxpayers across the\nstate. Prohibiting community colleges from charging the county of\nresidence of non-resident students for local sponsor costs for more\nthan two years will provide meaningful mandate relief to county\ntaxpayers.\n\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nTo be determined.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
